SPECS.garage = { name:"Garage Conversion", region:"England",
 cats:["Planning & Permitted Development","Applicability","General Notes","Structure","Foundations & Infill","Ground Floors","External Walls","Party Wall","Additional Notes for Walls","Roofs","Internal Works","Means of Escape & Fire Regs","Windows & Doors","Ventilation","Condensation (C2)","Drainage","Services","Conservation of Fuel & Power"],
 buildups:[
  {g:"FD", c:"Foundations & Infill", t:"Trench Fill Foundation", u:"Wall + 300 wide, 1000 deep",
   tgt:"To Approved Document A Section 2E and BS 8004:2015 (+A1:2020) — width from Table 10 for the load and subsoil, or the structural engineer's design",
   p:[
    "Mass concrete trench fill foundation beneath the new infill wall not less than 750mm thick, of a width not less than the thickness of the wall plus 300mm and in no case less than 450mm, taken to not less than 1000mm below finished ground level and cast against undisturbed ground in a trench excavated to the full width. Concrete to BS EN 206:2013 (+A2:2021) and BS 8500-2.",
    "Foundations supporting internal loadbearing walls to be taken to not less than 600mm below finished ground level. Foundations are to be taken below the invert of any adjacent drain and are not to undermine it. Sulfate-resisting cement, or the BS 8500 design chemical class given by the ground investigation, to be used where the ground or the groundwater requires it.",
    "Where the ground falls the foundation is to be stepped. No step is to exceed the thickness of the foundation, and the higher foundation is to overlap the lower by not less than twice the height of the step, the thickness of the foundation, or 300mm, whichever is the greater — not less than 1m where the foundation is trench fill. Masonry below ground to be stepped to suit.",
    "All work in accordance with Requirement A1/2 of the Building Regulations 2010 (as amended) and BS 8004:2015 (+A1:2020) Code of Practice for Foundations. The depth and the size of the foundation are to be approved on site by the Building Control Officer to suit the conditions found in the excavation.",
    "NOTE — The width is to be confirmed against Table 10 of Approved Document A for the wall load and the subsoil, or against the structural engineer's design. Where the wall is thin, the wall thickness plus 300mm can fall below the 450mm minimum for trench fill; the 450mm governs.",
    "NOTE — Where adverse soil conditions, a change of soil type, made ground, soft spots or major tree roots are found in the excavation, work is to stop, building control is to be notified and the structural engineer's advice obtained before any concrete is placed."
   ]},
  {g:"FD", c:"Foundations & Infill", t:"Strip Foundation", u:"Wall + 300 × 225, 1000 deep",
   tgt:"To Approved Document A Table 10 and Section 2E — thickness not less than the projection each side and not less than 225mm",
   p:[
    "Concrete strip foundation beneath the new infill wall with the width taken from Table 10 of Approved Document A for the wall load and the subsoil, not less than the thickness of the wall plus 300mm, and the thickness of the concrete not less than the projection of the foundation beyond each face of the wall and in no case less than 225mm. Concrete to BS EN 206:2013 (+A2:2021) and BS 8500-2, cast on undisturbed ground and taken to not less than 1000mm below finished ground level.",
    "Foundations supporting internal loadbearing walls to be taken to not less than 600mm below finished ground level. Foundations are to be taken below the invert of any adjacent drain and are not to undermine it. Sulfate-resisting cement, or the BS 8500 design chemical class given by the ground investigation, to be used where the ground or the groundwater requires it.",
    "Where the ground falls the foundation is to be stepped. No step is to exceed the thickness of the foundation, and the higher foundation is to overlap the lower by not less than twice the height of the step, the thickness of the foundation, or 300mm, whichever is the greater — not less than 1m where the foundation is trench fill. Masonry below ground to be stepped to suit.",
    "All work in accordance with Requirement A1/2 of the Building Regulations 2010 (as amended) and BS 8004:2015 (+A1:2020) Code of Practice for Foundations. The depth and the size of the foundation are to be approved on site by the Building Control Officer to suit the conditions found in the excavation.",
    "NOTE — The width is to be confirmed against Table 10 for the wall load and the subsoil, or against the structural engineer's design.",
    "NOTE — Where adverse soil conditions, a change of soil type, made ground, soft spots or major tree roots are found in the excavation, work is to stop, building control is to be notified and the structural engineer's advice obtained before any concrete is placed."
   ]},
  {g:"GF", c:"Ground Floors", t:"Existing Garage Slab — Insulation Over, Screed Finish", u:"0.17 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.25 W/m²K for the renovated floor (0.18 W/m²K where the floor is newly constructed)", mf:{"k":"floorSolid","lim":0.25,"p":{"ground":"clay","insulation":"tf70","thickness":100,"slab":"c100","finish":"screed65","edge":"e25x150","pa":0.8,"wall":300},"s":[{"r":"floor","f":"100mm Kingspan Thermafloor TF70 (thermal conductivity 0.022 W/mK)"}]}, p:[
    "Existing garage slab, drawn here as 100mm concrete and to be confirmed on site, to be retained where it is sound, level within 10mm over 3m and free of oil contamination. Clean back to a bare surface, remove any laitance and make good cracks and spalls with a cementitious repair mortar. Where the slab is fractured, sunken or contaminated it is to be broken out and replaced as GF3.",
    "Lay a polyethylene damp proof membrane of not less than 1200 gauge over the existing slab, lapped and taped at all joints and turned up the perimeter walls to lap with the damp proof course in the wall by not less than 100mm. Where no damp proof course exists in the garage walls, provide a new chemical or physical damp proof course at the correct level before the floor is laid.",
    "Provide 100mm Kingspan Thermafloor TF70 (thermal conductivity 0.022 W/mK) laid tightly butted over the membrane, a separating layer of 500 gauge polyethylene, 25mm perimeter upstand insulation to all external walls, and a 65mm sand and cement screed reinforced with a polypropylene fibre or D49 mesh. This build-up calculates at 0.17 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 13370 for a floor with a perimeter/area ratio of 0.8 on clay, which is typical of a single garage with two or three exposed sides.",
    "NOTE — Garage slabs are commonly 100 to 150mm below the house floor and were rarely laid on a damp proof membrane. The 190mm added by this build-up usually brings the finished floor to within a step of the house; where the levels do not work, use GF2 and accept the lower thermal performance, or break out and relay as GF3."]},
  {g:"GF", c:"Ground Floors", t:"Existing Garage Slab — Insulation Over, Timber Deck", u:"0.18 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.25 W/m²K for the renovated floor", mf:{"k":"floorSolid","lim":0.25,"p":{"ground":"clay","insulation":"k103","thickness":80,"slab":"c100","finish":"chip22","edge":"e25x150","pa":0.8,"wall":300},"s":[{"r":"floor","f":"80mm Kingspan Kooltherm K103 Floorboard (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK)"}]}, p:[
    "Existing slab, drawn here as 100mm concrete and to be confirmed on site, prepared and damp proof membrane laid as GF1. Provide 80mm Kingspan Kooltherm K103 Floorboard (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK) tightly butted over the membrane, a vapour control layer of 500 gauge polyethylene with laps taped, and 22mm tongued and grooved moisture resistant chipboard (P5) glued at all joints and laid as a floating floor with a 10mm expansion gap at the perimeter behind the skirting.",
    "Where the floor is to be raised further to meet the house floor level, use treated softwood battens at 400mm centres between insulation strips, with the deck screwed to the battens, or increase the insulation thickness; do not make up level with loose fill.",
    "This build-up calculates at 0.18 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 13370 for a perimeter/area ratio of 0.8 on clay."]},
  {g:"GF", c:"Ground Floors", t:"New Ground-Bearing Slab — Insulation Below Slab", u:"0.17 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.18 W/m²K for a new floor", mf:{"k":"floorSolid","lim":0.18,"p":{"ground":"clay","insulation":"tf70","thickness":100,"slab":"c100","finish":"screed65","edge":"e25x150","pa":0.8,"wall":300},"s":[{"r":"floor","f":"100mm Kingspan Thermafloor TF70"}]}, p:[
    "Where the existing slab is broken out, excavate to formation, remove all vegetable matter and lay 150mm minimum well compacted hardcore blinded with 50mm sand. Lay a polyethylene damp proof membrane of not less than 1200 gauge with all joints lapped and taped, linked to the damp proof course in the walls, 100mm Kingspan Thermafloor TF70 with 25mm perimeter upstands, and a 100mm minimum GEN1 concrete slab reinforced with A142 mesh, finished with a 65mm screed or a timber deck as GF2.",
    "The new slab is to be set out so the finished floor matches the adjoining house floor level. This build-up calculates at 0.17 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 13370 for a perimeter/area ratio of 0.8 on clay."]},
  {g:"EW", c:"Foundations & Infill", t:"Infill to Garage Door Opening — Cavity Wall", u:"0.17 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.18 W/m²K for a new wall", mf:{"k":"wall","lim":0.18,"p":{"outer":"brick","cavity":100,"fill":"full","insulation":"k106","thickness":90,"inner":"a011","mortar":"gp","finish":"pbdabs","ties":"ss45x90","gapLevel":1},"s":[{"r":"cavfull","f":"90mm Kingspan Kooltherm K106 Cavity Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK)"}]}, p:[
    "Remove the garage door, frame and any timber sub-frame. Expose and inspect the foundation beneath the opening: garage door thresholds are commonly carried on the slab thickening or on a shallow strip, and a new foundation to match the adjoining walls is to be provided where the existing is inadequate, to the structural engineer's direction, with the new footing stepped and bonded into the existing.",
    "New infill wall of 103mm facing brick to match the existing in colour, texture, bond and pointing, a 100mm cavity fully filled with 90mm Kingspan Kooltherm K106 Cavity Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK) leaving a nominal 10mm residual cavity, and a 100mm aircrete inner leaf of 0.11 W/mK thermal conductivity (Celcon Solar, Thermalite Turbo or equivalent), finished with 12.5mm plasterboard on dabs. Tooth the new brickwork into the existing at alternate courses or tie in with stainless steel wall starter profiles fixed at 225mm centres, with a vertical movement joint where the engineer requires one.",
    "Damp proof course at not less than 150mm above finished external ground level lapped with the floor membrane, insulated cavity closers at the jambs of the new window or door, a cavity tray with stop ends and weep holes over the new opening, and stainless steel wall ties at 450mm vertical and 900mm horizontal centres, staggered, with additional ties at not more than 300mm vertical centres within 225mm of the jambs (PD 6697).",
    "This build-up calculates at 0.17 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 allowing for mortar joints, ties and an air-gap correction of 0.01."]},
  {g:"EW", c:"Foundations & Infill", t:"Infill to Garage Door Opening — Timber Frame with Brick Outer Leaf", u:"0.16 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.18 W/m²K for a new wall", mf:{"k":"frame","lim":0.18,"p":{"insulation":"k112","thickness":140,"studWidth":38,"studDepth":140,"spacing":400,"sheathing":"osb9","lining":"k118_375","void":0,"gapLevel":1},"s":[{"r":"frame","f":"140mm Kingspan Kooltherm K112 Framing Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK)"}]}, p:[
    "Where the infill is to be built quickly or the foundation will only carry a light panel, form the infill as a timber frame panel behind a 103mm facing brick outer leaf tied to the frame, a 50mm clear cavity, breather membrane and 9mm OSB3 sheathing, on 140mm x 38mm C16 studs at 400mm centres with head and sole plates and noggins, the sole plate on a damp proof course and fixed to the slab or new plinth with resin anchors with stainless steel flexible ties at 600mm centres horizontally and 375mm vertically.",
    "Fill the full stud depth with 140mm Kingspan Kooltherm K112 Framing Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK) and line internally with 37.5mm insulated plasterboard incorporating a vapour control layer, joints taped. This build-up calculates at 0.16 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 by the combined method, allowing for the studs bridging the insulation at 9.5% of the area at 400mm centres and an air-gap correction of 0.01. Mineral wool of 0.035 W/mK in place of the phenolic board calculates at 0.20 and does not meet the standard for this panel.",
    "NOTE — A timber frame infill panel behind a brick skin is not a substitute for a foundation. The plinth and sole plate still need bearing below the level of frost and root action, and the engineer is to confirm the arrangement."]},
  {g:"EW", c:"Foundations & Infill", t:"Infill to Garage Door Opening — Solid Masonry with Internal Insulation", u:"0.17 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.18 W/m²K for a new wall", mf:{"k":"lined","lim":0.18,"p":{"wall":"brick215","gap":25,"insulation":"k112","thickness":100,"studWidth":50,"studDepth":100,"spacing":600,"lining":"k118_375","gapLevel":0},"s":[{"r":"frame","f":"100mm Kingspan Kooltherm K112 Framing Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK)"}]}, p:[
    "Where the existing garage walls are single-leaf and the infill is to read as the same wall, build the infill as 215mm solid brickwork bonded into the existing, or 100mm dense blockwork rendered to match, on the new foundation as EW1, with a damp proof course at 150mm above ground linked to the floor membrane and a proprietary insulated steel lintel or a concrete lintel over any opening. A solid wall cannot meet 0.18 on its own and is to be lined internally as the existing walls: treated softwood studs of 100mm x 50mm at 600mm centres set 25mm clear of the wall with 100mm Kingspan Kooltherm K112 Framing Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK) between and 37.5mm insulated plasterboard with a vapour control layer.",
    "The 215mm brick infill calculates at 0.17 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 allowing for the stud fraction and the rendered block version also at 0.17; with 90mm board in 89mm studs both calculate at 0.18 and meet the standard with no margin. Apply a liquid damp proof membrane to the internal face of the new masonry to 150mm above ground before the lining is fixed, and carry the lining and vapour control layer continuously round onto the existing walls so there is no cold strip at the junction.",
    "NOTE — A solid infill is only worth doing where the garage walls are solid and will be lined anyway; against a cavity-walled garage, use EW1 and keep the cavity continuous."]},
  {g:"EW", c:"External Walls", t:"Existing Single-Leaf Garage Wall — New Inner Leaf Forming a Cavity Wall", u:"0.24 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.30 W/m²K for a retained wall becoming part of the thermal envelope (0.18 where the new leaf is treated as a new wall)",
   p:[
    "Where floor area allows and the client wants a masonry room rather than a lined one, retain the existing wall as the outer leaf, drawn here as 100mm block, leave a 100mm cavity fully filled with 100mm Knauf DriTherm Cavity Slab 32 or ROCKWOOL Full Fill Cavity Batt, and build a new 100mm aircrete inner leaf of 0.11 W/mK on a thickening to the existing slab or a new strip footing to the engineer's design of 0.037 W/mK, tied to the existing wall with stainless steel remedial ties drilled and resin fixed at 450mm vertical and 900mm horizontal centres, and finished with 12.5mm plasterboard on dabs.",
    "Damp proof course in the new leaf at the level of the existing damp proof course or the new floor membrane, whichever is higher, cavity trays and stop ends over openings, insulated cavity closers at the jambs, and the cavity closed at the head with a cavity barrier. With the 0.032 W/mK slab the wall calculates at 0.24 W/m²K and with the 0.037 batt at 0.26 to BS EN ISO 6946, both meeting the retained-element standard; a 150mm cavity with 150mm of the 0.032 slab calculates at 0.18 where the new-wall value is wanted.",
    "NOTE — The new leaf costs about 200mm of room width each side. On a single garage that is the difference between a bedroom and a box room; check the plan before choosing it over the stud lining."]},
  {g:"EW", c:"External Walls", t:"Existing Uninsulated Cavity Wall — Blown Cavity Insulation", u:"0.28 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.30 W/m²K for a retained wall becoming part of the thermal envelope",
   p:[
    "Where the garage was built with a clear cavity in sound condition, drawn here as a 103mm brick outer leaf, a 50mm blown mineral wool cavity fill and a 100mm dense block inner leaf, have the cavity surveyed with a borescope and, where it is clean, dry and free of bridging and the wall is not in a severely exposed location, fill it with a blown mineral wool, bonded polystyrene bead or urea-formaldehyde foam system holding a BBA certificate for the wall type, installed by a registered installer to BS EN 14064 or the certificate, with a CIGA or equivalent guarantee, injection holes made good and vents and flues sleeved before filling. Line internally with 52.5mm insulated plasterboard on dabs where the cavity is 50mm, or 12.5mm plasterboard on dabs where the cavity is 100mm or more.",
    "A 103mm brick, 50mm blown bead (0.033 W/mK) and 100mm dense block wall with plain plasterboard on dabs calculates at 0.47 W/m²K and does not meet the standard on its own; with 37.5mm insulated plasterboard it calculates at 0.31 and still fails, and with 52.5mm insulated plasterboard at 0.25 and passes. A 100mm cavity blown full with plain plasterboard on dabs calculates at 0.28. The installer's survey is to be kept with the completion certificate.",
    "NOTE — Blown insulation is not to be used where the outer leaf is single-leaf render on block, where the pointing is defective, or where the cavity contains debris; those walls are to be lined as the stud or board build-ups instead."]},
  {g:"EW", c:"External Walls", t:"Existing Garage Wall — External Wall Insulation", u:"0.29 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.30 W/m²K for a retained wall becoming part of the thermal envelope",
   p:[
    "Where internal space is critical and the elevation is not constrained by planning, insulate the existing wall externally, drawn here as a 100mm single-leaf block wall and to be confirmed on site, with 100mm expanded polystyrene of 0.032 W/mK or 100mm mineral wool of 0.036 W/mK mechanically fixed and adhered to the prepared masonry, a base coat with alkali-resistant mesh and a through-coloured silicone render finish, as a complete BBA-certificated system installed by the system holder's approved contractor, with a starter track and drip above the damp proof course, beads at corners and openings, sills extended to give a drip over the new face, and the eaves, verge and rainwater goods extended to clear the added thickness.",
    "The 100mm expanded polystyrene system on a 100mm dense block wall calculates at 0.29 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 and on a 215mm brick wall at 0.27; the 100mm mineral wool version calculates at 0.32 on the block wall and fails, and 0.30 on the brick wall with no margin, so where mineral wool is required (within 1m of the boundary) it is to be 120mm. External wall insulation to a house or garage elevation is development needing planning permission on most elevations and is not to be specified without it."]},
  {g:"EW", c:"External Walls", t:"Existing Single-Leaf Garage Wall — Insulated Stud Lining", u:"0.24 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.30 W/m²K for a retained wall becoming part of the thermal envelope", mf:{"k":"lined","lim":0.3,"p":{"wall":"block100","gap":25,"insulation":"k112","thickness":89,"studWidth":38,"studDepth":89,"spacing":600,"lining":"none","gapLevel":0},"s":[{"r":"frame","f":"90mm Kingspan Kooltherm K112 Framing Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK)"}]}, p:[
    "Existing 100mm block or 215mm brick single-leaf wall to be retained. Inspect externally for cracked, hollow or missing pointing and render and make good; the external face is the only rain screen the wall has. Apply a liquid damp proof membrane or a proprietary tanking slurry to the internal face from floor level to 150mm above external ground level, lapped with the floor membrane.",
    "Line internally with treated softwood studs of 89mm x 38mm at 600mm centres, set clear of the wall on a damp proof course strip with a 25mm clear gap behind them, and 90mm Kingspan Kooltherm K112 Framing Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK) between the studs, a continuous vapour control layer on the warm side with laps taped, and 12.5mm plasterboard skimmed. Carry the insulation and vapour control layer behind the floor deck and up to the ceiling line without breaks.",
    "This build-up calculates at 0.24 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 allowing for the stud fraction at 600mm centres. Mineral wool of 0.035 W/mK in the same 89mm stud calculates at 0.34 and does not meet the standard; where mineral wool is preferred the studs are to be 140mm deep and fully filled, which calculates at 0.27. As an alternative where floor area is critical, 72.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard fixed on battens over the damp proofed wall calculates at 0.27 W/m²K.",
    "NOTE — A single-leaf garage wall was never designed to keep a habitable room dry. Where the wall is exposed to driving rain or the ground is high on the outside, the lining is to be a cavity drainage or ventilated system and the client advised in writing before construction."]},
  {g:"EW", c:"External Walls", t:"Existing Cavity Wall — Insulated Plasterboard", u:"0.29 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.30 W/m²K for a retained wall becoming part of the thermal envelope", mf:{"k":"lined","lim":0.3,"p":{"wall":"cavity","gap":10,"lining":"k118_625","gapLevel":0},"s":[{"r":"lining","f":"62.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard"}]}, p:[
    "Where the garage was built with an uninsulated cavity wall, inspect the cavity with a borescope for debris and bridging, clean and confirm the damp proof course. Line the internal face of the existing wall, drawn here as a 103mm brick outer leaf, a 50mm clear cavity and a 100mm dense block inner leaf, with 62.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard on dabs with a continuous ribbon of adhesive at the perimeter and around openings and all joints taped. As an alternative the cavity may be filled with a certificated blown insulation and the wall lined with 37.5mm insulated plasterboard.",
    "The 62.5mm board on dabs calculates at 0.29 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 for a 103mm brick outer leaf, 50mm clear cavity and 100mm dense block inner leaf. Where the inner leaf is aircrete of 0.15 W/mK the board may be reduced to 52.5mm, which also calculates at 0.29."]},
  {g:"EW", c:"Party Wall", t:"Existing Garage Wall Shared with the Neighbour", u:"0.27 W/m²K / REI 60",
   tgt:"To maintain 60 minutes fire resistance to a wall separating two buildings, and to insulate it to 0.30 W/m²K where the neighbour's side remains an unheated garage", mf:{"k":"lined","lim":0.3,"p":{"wall":"block100","internalBoth":true,"gap":10,"lining":"k118_725","gapLevel":0},"s":[{"r":"lining","f":"72.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard"}],"alt":{"k":"lined","lim":0.3,"p":{"wall":"block100","internalBoth":true,"gap":25,"insulation":"k112","thickness":100,"studWidth":38,"studDepth":100,"spacing":600,"lining":"none","gapLevel":1},"s":[{"r":"frame","f":"100mm Kingspan Kooltherm K112 Framing Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK)"}]}}, p:[
    "Where the garage is one of a pair or a terrace and the wall between is shared, the wall is a compartment wall separating two buildings: make good every gap, open perpend, redundant hole and built-in timber so the wall gives 60 minutes fire resistance, carry it up to the underside of the roof covering and fire stop the junction, and do not chase sockets or recesses into it. The neighbour's garage is an unheated space, so the wall is a thermal element of the new room and is to be insulated to 0.30 W/m²K: 72.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard on dabs or battens to the existing wall, drawn here as a 100mm single-leaf block wall. That calculates at 0.27 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 with both faces treated as internal surfaces, and to a 215mm brick wall at 0.26; 62.5mm calculates at 0.31 and 0.30 and is not to be used.",
    "As an alternative, where the insulated plasterboard range chosen does not reach the standard in the thickness it is made in, or where a deeper lining is acceptable, line the wall with 100mm x 38mm treated softwood studs at 600mm centres set 25mm clear of the shared wall and fixed to the floor and the ceiling so that no fixing penetrates it, the stud depth filled with 100mm Kingspan Kooltherm K112 Framing Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK) or a 0.022 W/mK board of the same thickness, a continuous vapour control layer on the warm side with laps taped, and 12.5mm plasterboard skimmed. With the phenolic board the lining calculates at 0.23 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 on the 100mm block wall and 0.22 on a 215mm brick wall; with a 0.022 W/mK board at 0.25 and 0.24; and with mineral wool of 0.035 W/mK at 0.33, which does not meet the standard. The lining takes more room width than the insulated plasterboard on dabs, which is to be checked against the finished room before it is adopted.",
    "Where the neighbour has already converted and heats the room on the other side, the wall is not a thermal element and needs no insulation, but the fire treatment still applies and an independent lining with mineral wool is recommended for sound. Where the shared wall is a cavity wall, the cavity is to be closed at the top and at the ends with a cavity barrier so a garage fire cannot pass through it, and a blown cavity fill may be used where both owners agree.",
    "Cutting into, raising or lining a shared wall is notifiable under the Party Wall etc. Act 1996 (two months' notice under Section 2 for cutting in; a lining fixed with plugs is not normally notifiable but a schedule of condition is prudent)."]},
  {g:"IW", c:"Internal Works", t:"New Wall Between Retained Garage and Habitable Room", u:"REI 30",
   tgt:"To achieve 30 minutes fire resistance where part of the garage is retained for parking",
   p:[
    "Where the front part of the garage is retained as a garage, the new dividing wall is to be 100mm dense concrete blockwork off a new thickening or strip foundation, plastered both sides, or a stud partition of 89mm x 38mm studs at 400mm centres with 100mm mineral wool infill of 33 kg/m³ minimum and two layers of 12.5mm plasterboard each side, joints staggered, to give 30 minutes fire resistance from the garage side.",
    "Any door between the garage and the habitable room is to be an FD30S fire doorset (E 30 Sa, with intumescent strips and cold smoke seals) fitted with a self-closing device, with the threshold not less than 100mm above the garage floor or the garage floor laid to fall away from the door to the vehicle entrance. Carry the wall up to the underside of the roof covering and fire stop the head.",
    "The retained garage is unheated, so this wall is also a new thermal element of the dwelling and is to achieve 0.18 W/m²K: for the blockwork option build it as a cavity wall of a 100mm dense block leaf on the garage side and a 100mm aircrete leaf of 0.11 W/mK on the room side with a 150mm cavity fully filled with mineral wool of 0.032 W/mK, which calculates at 0.16 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 (a 100mm cavity calculates at 0.21 and a single block leaf with 72.5mm insulated plasterboard at 0.27, both of which meet only the retained-element value); for the stud option use 140mm studs filled with Kingspan Kooltherm K112 with 37.5mm insulated plasterboard on the room side, which calculates at 0.15, or 89mm studs with K112 and 52.5mm insulated plasterboard at 0.17. A vapour control layer on the room side in every case.",
    "NOTE — This separation is only required where a garage remains. Where the whole garage is converted, the former separation between garage and house no longer has a function and the 100mm step at the connecting door may be removed and the floor levelled."]},
  {g:"RF", c:"Roofs", t:"Existing Flat Roof — Warm Deck Overlay", u:"0.15 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.16 W/m²K for a renovated roof (Approved Document L Volume 1 Table 4.3 gives one value for all roof types)", mf:{"k":"flat","lim":0.16,"p":{"insulation":"tr26","thickness":130,"joistDepth":150,"spacing":400,"fixing":"adhered"},"s":[{"r":"flat","f":"130mm Kingspan Thermaroof TR26 LPC/FM (thermal conductivity 0.022 W/mK)"}]}, p:[
    "Strip the existing felt or replace the decking where it is soft, delaminated or sagging; existing chipboard decks are to be replaced, over the existing joists, drawn here as 47mm x 200mm joists at 400mm centres and to be confirmed on site, with 18mm exterior grade plywood or OSB3 laid to falls. Lay a vapour control layer fully bonded to the deck with all laps sealed, 130mm Kingspan Thermaroof TR26 LPC/FM (thermal conductivity 0.022 W/mK) fully adhered, and a single ply or high performance built-up felt waterproofing system to the manufacturer's specification and BBA certificate, with upstands of not less than 150mm at all abutments and new edge trims.",
    "Where the roof joists are undersized for the new loads or the room below, they are to be strengthened to the structural engineer's design. Existing cross ventilation to the joist void is to be sealed once the warm deck is complete so the void is enclosed on the warm side of the insulation.",
    "This build-up calculates at 0.15 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 fully adhered with 150mm joists. Where headroom at the fascia does not allow 130mm, 120mm calculates at 0.17 and 100mm at 0.20, neither of which meets the renovation standard of 0.16 without a compensating measure agreed with building control."]},
  {g:"RF", c:"Roofs", t:"Existing Flat Roof — Insulation Between Joists (Cold Roof, Covering Retained)", u:"0.15 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.16 W/m²K for a renovated roof", mf:{"k":"rafter","lim":0.16,"p":{"insulation":"k107","thickness":100,"rafterDepth":150,"spacing":400,"under":"k118_725","gapLevel":1},"s":[{"r":"rafter","f":"100mm Kingspan Kooltherm K107 or K103 board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK)"},{"r":"lining","f":"72.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard"}]}, p:[
    "Where the existing flat roof covering is sound and is to be retained, insulate between the joists from below with 100mm Kingspan Kooltherm K107 or K103 board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK) held tightly between 150mm joists so that a 50mm ventilated void remains between the insulation and the deck, and fix 72.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard with an integral vapour control layer to the underside of the joists with joints taped. The void above the insulation is to be cross-ventilated by continuous 25mm openings along two opposite eaves, with the airflow not blocked by the wall plate or the fascia, and the vapour control layer is to be continuous and sealed at the walls and around every penetration.",
    "This build-up calculates at 0.15 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 allowing for joist bridging at 400mm centres and the 0.01 air-gap correction; 62.5mm beneath calculates at 0.17, 37.5mm at 0.22 and plain plasterboard at 0.31, none of which meets the 0.16 standard. A cold roof depends on the ventilation and the vapour control layer; where either cannot be achieved, or the covering has less than five years' life, use the warm deck overlay instead.",
    "NOTE — Building control will normally accept a cold roof only where the covering is not being renewed. Once the felt comes off, Regulation 23 makes the warm deck the expected answer."]},
  {g:"RF", c:"Roofs", t:"Existing Pitched Roof — Insulation at Rafter Level (Room in Roof)", u:"0.15 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.16 W/m²K for a renovated roof (0.15 W/m²K where the roof is rebuilt as a new element)", mf:{"k":"rafter","lim":0.16,"p":{"insulation":"k107","thickness":100,"rafterDepth":150,"spacing":400,"under":"k118_725","gapLevel":1},"s":[{"r":"rafter","f":"100mm Kingspan Kooltherm K107 Pitched Roof Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK)"},{"r":"lining","f":"72.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard"}]}, p:[
    "Where the garage roof space is to be used or the ceiling follows the roof slope, insulate at rafter level: 100mm Kingspan Kooltherm K107 Pitched Roof Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK) fitted tightly between the rafters, deepened to 125mm or 150mm by counter-battens or bolted deepening pieces where the existing rafters are shallow so that a 50mm ventilated gap remains above the board under a non-breathable underlay (BS 5250), and 72.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard beneath the rafters with joints staggered and taped and a continuous vapour control layer.",
    "This build-up calculates at 0.15 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 at 400mm rafter centres allowing for rafter bridging and an air-gap correction of 0.01; 62.5mm beneath calculates at 0.17 and 52.5mm at 0.18, neither of which meets the 0.16 renovation standard. Ventilate the void above the insulation at the eaves and ridge equivalent to 25mm and 5mm continuous gaps unless the underlay is breathable and the covering is on counter-battens."]},
  {g:"RF", c:"Roofs", t:"Existing Pitched Roof — Insulation at Ceiling Level", u:"0.15 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.16 W/m²K for a renovated pitched roof insulated at ceiling level",
   p:[
    "Provide a new ceiling of 12.5mm plasterboard with a vapour control layer where none exists, and lay 100mm mineral wool quilt of 0.044 W/mK between the ceiling joists with a further 200mm laid cross-wise over the joists, to a total of 300mm. Maintain a continuous 25mm ventilation path at the eaves with proprietary eaves ventilators and insulation stops, and provide ventilation equivalent to a continuous 10mm gap along two opposite eaves, or 25mm where the pitch is below 15 degrees.",
    "Insulate and draught seal any access hatch, and carry the insulation over the wall plate to meet the wall insulation without a gap. This build-up calculates at 0.15 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 allowing for joist bridging at 400mm centres, meeting the 0.16 renovation standard with little to spare; do not reduce the quilt thickness."]},
  {g:"RF", c:"Roofs", t:"New Flat Roof — Warm Deck", u:"0.15 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.15 W/m²K for a new roof", mf:{"k":"flat","lim":0.15,"p":{"insulation":"tr27","thickness":150,"joistDepth":200,"spacing":400,"fixing":"adhered"},"s":[{"r":"flat","f":"150mm Kingspan Thermaroof TR27 LPC/FM (thermal conductivity 0.024 W/mK at 120mm and over)"}]}, p:[
    "Where the garage roof is renewed in full, provide 47mm x 200mm C24 joists at 400mm centres to the structural engineer's design, 18mm exterior grade plywood or OSB3 decking laid to falls of not less than 1:40 finished, a vapour control layer fully bonded to the deck, 150mm Kingspan Thermaroof TR27 LPC/FM (thermal conductivity 0.024 W/mK at 120mm and over) fully adhered, and a single ply or built-up felt waterproofing system to the manufacturer's specification and BBA certificate with 150mm upstands and edge trims.",
    "This build-up calculates at 0.15 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 fully adhered; where mechanically fixed use 160mm."]}
 ],
 notes:[
  {c:"Planning & Permitted Development", t:"Planning Status", p:[
    "Conversion of an attached or integral garage to a habitable room is normally permitted development where the external appearance is not materially altered and no planning condition restricts it. Check the original permission for the dwelling or estate: a condition requiring the garage to be retained for parking is common on estates built since the 1980s and removes permitted development for the conversion.",
    "Where the conversion changes the front elevation with a window or door, the work is to comply with the Class A conditions for materials of similar appearance. Conversion of a detached garage to a self-contained dwelling or annexe with independent living accommodation is not permitted development and requires planning permission.",
    "Building Regulations approval does not confer planning permission, and planning permission does not confer Building Regulations approval. Where the property is in a conservation area, listed or subject to an Article 4 Direction, separate consent is to be confirmed before work starts."]},
  {c:"Applicability", t:"How the Regulations Apply to the Conversion", p:[
    "Conversion of an attached garage to a habitable room is a material alteration under Regulation 3 and, because the garage walls, floor and roof become part of the thermal envelope of the dwelling, the work is a change to the building's energy status under Regulation 22, and Regulation 23 applies to each of those thermal elements that is renovated or replaced in the process (re-covering the roof, relaying the slab). Approved Document L Volume 1 Section 4 therefore applies: each retained element is to be brought to the standard in Table 4.3 of the Approved Document where it is technically, functionally and economically feasible, which for a garage conversion it invariably is, and each new element (the infill wall, any new floor or roof) to Table 4.2.",
    "Conversion of a detached garage into a self-contained dwelling is a material change of use under Regulation 5(a) and brings in the fuller list of requirements in Regulation 6, including structure, sound, access and the whole-dwelling requirements. This specification covers the conversion of a garage to a habitable room forming part of the existing dwelling; where a separate dwelling is created, use the New Build specification for the applicable requirements.",
    "NOTE — Building control will expect the conversion to be notified either by a Building Notice or a Full Plans application before work begins. A garage conversion carried out without notification cannot be regularised at the end without opening up, and mortgage lenders and purchasers ask for the completion certificate."]},
  {c:"General Notes", t:"Compliance and Scope", p:[
    "All work to be carried out in accordance with the Building Regulations 2010 as amended, the current Approved Documents and the British Standards referred to in them. Where a British Standard has been superseded, the current edition applies. All materials to be fit for their purpose and installed in accordance with the manufacturer's instructions and any third-party certificate.",
    "These notes and build-ups are to be read together with the drawings and the structural engineer's calculations. Where there is a conflict the drawings take precedence for dimensions and the engineer's information for structure. Any discrepancy is to be referred to the designer before the work proceeds.",
    "Existing construction described in this specification is assumed from the survey. Where opening up reveals a different construction, the affected build-ups are to be reviewed before work continues."]},
  {c:"General Notes", t:"Statutory Notices (Regulation 16), Inspections and Party Wall", p:[
    "Regulation 16 of the Building Regulations 2010 (as amended in October 2023) requires three notices to the building control body: notice of intention to start work, given so that at least two clear days elapse before work begins; a commencement notice within five days of the work being regarded as commenced under Regulation 46A, which for a new building or a horizontal extension is when the sub-structure including the ground floor structure is complete and for other work (loft, garage, basement and flat conversions) is when 15 per cent of the work is complete, after which the authority has four weeks to reject the notice; and a completion notice within five days of completion. The completion notice must give the name, address, telephone number and email of the client, the principal contractor and the principal designer, state that the work is complete, include a signed statement by the client that the work complies with the Building Regulations, and include signed statements from the principal designer and principal contractor confirming that they have fulfilled their duties under Part 2A with the dates of their appointments; where a statement cannot be obtained the reason is to be given.",
    "Inspection stages to be notified as they arise: the foundation excavation under the garage door opening before concrete is placed and the foundation before it is covered, the damp proof course and floor membrane, structural steel and the fire-resisting separation to any retained garage before it is closed up, the insulation before it is boarded over, drains before backfilling and on test, and completion. Certificates from competent person schemes (electrical, gas, replacement windows, heating appliances) and commissioning notices for fixed building services and ventilation are to be given to building control within the periods the Regulations set (normally five days) and copied to the client with the completion certificate, which mortgage lenders and purchasers' solicitors will ask for.",
    "Where the garage is attached to a neighbour's garage or house, work to the shared wall (cutting in for beams or lintels, raising it, or fixing a lining with mechanical fixings that penetrate it) is notifiable under Section 2 of the Party Wall etc. Act 1996 with two months' notice, and a new foundation within 3m of the neighbour's building and deeper than theirs is notifiable under Section 6 with one month's notice. Serve the notices when the engineer's design is settled and before the contractor is booked.",
    "NOTE — The two-day and five-day limbs are separate duties. Missing the commencement notice means the work is not treated as commenced for transitional purposes, which matters where an edition of the Approved Documents is about to change."]},
  {c:"General Notes", t:"Existing Construction to be Verified", p:[
    "Before the design is finalised the contractor is to establish the construction of the garage walls (single leaf or cavity, insulated or not), the presence and level of any damp proof course, the presence of a damp proof membrane under the slab, the depth and condition of the foundation under the garage door threshold, the condition of the roof deck and joists, and the route of any drainage or services passing under or through the garage.",
    "Findings are to be reported to the designer with photographs. A short trial hole at the garage door threshold and a cored sample of the slab are normally sufficient."]},
  {c:"Structure", t:"Structural Design — General", p:[
    "All structural work to be to the design and details of the structural engineer and to the engineer's satisfaction on site. Structural calculations and details are to be submitted to building control before the relevant work begins, and no structural element is to be loaded before the engineer has confirmed the supporting construction.",
    "Where the roof of an integral garage supports a room above, removal of the garage door lintel or any pier is to be checked for its effect on the structure above, and needles and props provided while the new support is installed."]},
  {c:"Structure", t:"Lintels, Beams, New Openings and Fire Protection", p:[
    "Lintels over new openings, including the new window or door in the infill wall, to be proprietary steel lintels to BS EN 845-2, insulated where they cross a cavity and with a cavity tray, stop ends and weep holes above, or precast prestressed concrete lintels, sized from the manufacturer's tables for the span and load with not less than 150mm end bearing on a full block or padstone; the existing garage door lintel is to be checked for its condition and bearing before the infill is built beneath it and replaced where corroded or undersized. Steel beams to BS EN 10025 on padstones to the engineer's design, primed and, where they form part of the floor over the garage or the separation to a retained garage, protected to give 30 minutes fire resistance by two layers of 12.5mm plasterboard or one of 15mm fire-resisting board with joints staggered, or an intumescent coating to the certified thickness.",
    "Where a new opening is formed between the garage and the house, the wall is to be temporarily supported on needles and props and a new lintel or steel beam provided to the engineer's design with padstones of the size specified, bearing not less than 150mm each end, the reveals made good and the fire protection above applied where the beam forms part of a floor or wall that must resist fire."]},
  {c:"Foundations & Infill", t:"Foundation Under the Garage Door Opening and Fallback Foundation Rules", p:[
    "The foundation under the garage door threshold is to be exposed and its depth and width recorded. Where it is shallower than the adjoining wall foundations or less than 750mm below external ground level in clay, a new concrete strip foundation of GEN1 concrete, not less than 600mm wide and 1000mm deep or to the engineer's design, is to be provided beneath the new infill wall and bonded to the existing with the step not exceeding the thickness of the footing.",
    "Where the structural engineer is not designing the foundations and the ground is a firm clay, sand or gravel without trees, fill or soft spots, Approved Document A Section 2E gives the fallback: strip foundations of GEN1 concrete not less than 225mm thick with the width from Table 10 for the wall load and soil type (not less than the wall thickness plus 300mm in most domestic cases, and never less than the projection each side equalling the thickness), taken to not less than 1000mm below finished ground in clay and 750mm elsewhere, with internal loadbearing walls on foundations not less than 600mm deep; trench-fill foundations not less than 450mm wide; steps in the foundation not exceeding its thickness with the higher foundation overlapping the lower by not less than twice the step, the thickness or 300mm whichever is greater (1m for trench fill). Foundations to be taken below the invert of any adjacent drain and not to be undermined by it, with the drain sleeved or bridged with 50mm clearance and flexible joints each side where it passes through; sulfate-resisting cement or the BS 8500 designated class where the ground or groundwater requires it.",
    "Where trees are within influencing distance in shrinkable clay, foundation depths are to follow NHBC Chapter 4.2 and the engineer's recommendation with heave precautions where required; where the ground is filled, soft or variable, a raft or piled foundation to the engineer's design is used instead, with raft reinforcement lapped 450mm and 40mm cover. All excavations to be inspected by building control before concrete is placed."]},
  {c:"Foundations & Infill", t:"Damp Proof Course and Ground Levels", p:[
    "A damp proof course is to be provided in the new infill wall at not less than 150mm above finished external ground level and linked to the floor membrane. External ground, paving and drive levels are to be reduced where they are within 150mm of the damp proof course, and the driveway falls set so surface water does not run to the new wall. Where the drive level cannot be lowered, a linear drainage channel connected to the surface water system is to be provided across the former garage door threshold."]},
  {c:"Ground Floors", t:"Radon and Ground Gas", p:[
    "Where the property is in a radon affected area on the UK radon map, the conversion is to include protection to BR 211 because a habitable room is being created at ground level over an existing slab that has none: basic protection is a radon-proof membrane of a certificated grade in place of the standard damp proof membrane, sealed at every lap and penetration and taken through the walls to link with the damp proof course; full protection, where the map shows the higher probability band, adds a means of subfloor depressurisation, normally a radon sump beneath the slab with a capped pipe to the outside for a fan to be added later. Where the site is on filled ground or near former industrial use, gas protection to BS 8485 is to be considered. The radon status of the postcode is to be checked at design stage and recorded on the drawings."]},
  {c:"Ground Floors", t:"Other Existing Floors and Vapour Control", p:[
    "Where the garage floor is found to be a suspended timber or beam and block floor rather than a ground-bearing slab, it is to be insulated as a suspended floor: rigid board between or over the structure with the void beneath kept ventilated by air bricks on opposite walls, a damp proof membrane over a beam and block deck, and a breathable membrane over timber joists. A vapour control layer is to be provided above the insulation of every floor build-up in this section, lapped up the walls behind the skirting and taped to the wall damp proofing, so that moisture from the slab cannot reach the deck or screed."]},
  {c:"Ground Floors", t:"Floor Level and Junction with the House", p:[
    "The finished floor level of the new room is to match the adjoining house floor where practicable. Where a step remains it is to be a single step of not more than 220mm with a nosing clearly distinguishable, and noted on the drawings. Where a level connection is made, the former garage floor is to be checked for falls to the door opening and levelled.",
    "The floor membrane is to be continuous with the wall damp proof course around the full perimeter, including the party or house wall where the garage is integral."]},
  {c:"Additional Notes for Walls", t:"Movement Joints and Attaching New to Existing", p:[
    "Movement joints in the new infill masonry where a clay brick panel exceeds 12m or a concrete block panel exceeds 6m, or where the new work meets the existing at a straight joint, formed with a compressible filler and a low-modulus sealant with debonded ties across the joint in the inner leaf. Where the new infill is tied rather than toothed into the existing, stainless steel wall starter profiles fixed to the existing masonry at 225mm centres with the ties built into every course of the new leaf, and the joint between new and existing pointed with a mastic sealant where a straight joint is expressed. New blockwork inner leaves to be built in stretcher bond with bed and perpend joints full, and not built more than 1.5m in a day."]},
  {c:"External Walls", t:"External Finish and Weathering", p:[
    "The new infill brickwork is to match the existing elevation in brick, bond, mortar colour and joint profile, with the courses set out to align. Where an exact match is not available the elevation is to be rendered or the infill set back and expressed as a panel, subject to planning. Provide a new sill with a projecting drip to the new window, and make good the external plinth and any render."]},
  {c:"External Walls", t:"Cavity Barriers and Closers", p:[
    "Close the cavity of the new infill wall at the jambs, head and sill of openings with proprietary insulated cavity closers, and at the junction with the existing walls with a mineral wool cavity barrier the full depth of the cavity. Where the garage is integral and the new room is below a bedroom, cavity barriers are to be provided in the external wall cavity at the line of the floor above where they are absent."]},
  {c:"Roofs", t:"Roof Structure and Ventilation", p:[
    "Existing garage roofs are commonly built with joists sized for a garage rather than a room, and with no allowance for a ceiling load, insulation and a warm deck build-up. The engineer is to confirm the joists or specify strengthening. Where a flat roof is upgraded to a warm deck, the joist void becomes unventilated and existing soffit vents are to be closed; where the roof remains a cold roof the void is to be cross-ventilated with a continuous 25mm gap at opposite edges."]},
  {c:"Roofs", t:"Rainwater Goods", p:[
    "Existing gutters and downpipes to be checked for falls and discharge, cleared and repaired. Where the new room is below a garage roof that drains to a gully in the drive, the gully is to be retained and kept clear; where it drains over the new door or window, a new downpipe and connection to the surface water drain or a soakaway to BRE Digest 365 is to be provided."]},
  {c:"Internal Works", t:"Partitions and the Existing Ceiling", p:[
    "New internal partitions within the converted room of 89mm x 38mm timber studs or 70mm metal C studs at 600mm centres with 12.5mm plasterboard each side and mineral wool infill where they separate a bedroom or WC from another room (40 dB Rw to Requirement E2), or 100mm aircrete blockwork off a thickening to the slab where a masonry partition is preferred, plastered or lined. Where the garage ceiling is retained beneath a room above, upgrade it to 30 minutes fire resistance where the room above is a bedroom or the ceiling forms part of the separation to a retained garage, by two layers of 12.5mm plasterboard or one of 15mm fire-resisting board with joints staggered, with 100mm mineral wool in the void for sound between the new room and the room above."]},
  {c:"Internal Works", t:"Internal Finishes", p:[
    "New plasterboard to be skimmed or taped and jointed and finished to receive decoration. Skirtings, architraves and door linings to match the existing house. Where the former garage ceiling is exposed rafters or joists, a new plasterboard ceiling of 12.5mm board with a vapour control layer is to be provided."]},
  {c:"Means of Escape & Fire Regs", t:"Means of Escape from the New Room", p:[
    "The new ground-storey habitable room is to have either a door opening directly to the outside or an emergency egress window with an unobstructed openable area of not less than 0.33m² and not less than 450mm in both height and width, with the bottom of the openable area between 800mm and 1100mm above floor level. Where the room is an inner room reached only through another room, the egress window or door is mandatory; a kitchen is not to be the access room to an inner room.",
    "Where the conversion creates a bedroom that opens off a hall serving the stair, the hall is to lead to the front door without passing through another room."]},
  {c:"Means of Escape & Fire Regs", t:"Smoke, Heat and Carbon Monoxide Alarms", p:[
    "Extend the existing fire detection and alarm system, or where none exists or it is battery-only provide a new system throughout the dwelling, to BS 5839-6 Grade D1 Category LD2 — mains powered, interlinked smoke alarms to BS EN 14604 with an integral tamper-proof standby supply — in the circulation space on every storey and within 7.5m of every habitable room door, in the principal habitable room and in the new room where it is an inner room or a bedroom, with a heat alarm to BS 5446-2 in the kitchen. This exceeds the Grade D2 Category LD3 minimum given in Approved Document B Volume 1 and is adopted as this practice's standard so that the whole house is protected, not only the new room. Alarms to be ceiling mounted not less than 300mm from walls and light fittings and supplied from an independent circuit at the consumer unit or from a regularly used local lighting circuit, with a carbon monoxide alarm to BS EN 50291 in every room containing a fixed combustion appliance. The design, installation and commissioning certificate for the system to be given to building control on completion.",
    "Where the dwelling is or will be let, the Smoke and Carbon Monoxide Alarm (England) Regulations 2015 as amended in 2022 require at least one smoke alarm on every storey with living accommodation and a carbon monoxide alarm in every room with a fixed combustion appliance other than a gas cooker, and the new room is to be brought within the interlinked Grade D1 system with the landlord given the commissioning certificate. Where the room becomes a bedroom in a house in multiple occupation, the LACORS guidance and the licence conditions govern the grade and category and will normally require an alarm in the room itself and a fire door to it."]},
  {c:"Means of Escape & Fire Regs", t:"Fire Resistance and Separation", p:[
    "Where the whole garage is converted the former fire separation between garage and dwelling ceases to have a function. Where any part of the garage is retained as a garage the separation in build-up IW1 applies and is to be continuous to the underside of the roof covering, with the ceiling over the retained garage of 30 minutes fire resistance where there is a room above. Where the converted room is within 1m of the relevant boundary, or the garage is detached and the conversion creates an annexe, the wall, unprotected area and roof covering provisions in the External Fire Spread note apply."]},
  {c:"Windows & Doors", t:"New Windows and External Doors", p:[
    "New windows to have a whole-window U-value not exceeding 1.4 W/m²K, and new external doors 1.4 W/m²K, in accordance with Approved Document L Volume 1 Table 4.2. Frames to be fitted with trickle ventilators providing not less than 8000mm² equivalent area to the new habitable room (10,000mm² where the dwelling is single-storey), and to be draught stripped. Sills to have a projecting drip; the cavity is to be closed at the jambs and the frame sealed to the closer.",
    "Glazing in critical locations — any glazing within 800mm of floor level in a window or wall, within 1500mm of floor level in a door or within 300mm of a door edge — is to be safety glass to BS EN 12600 Class 2 or better and permanently marked. Where the new room is a bedroom, at least one window is to be an egress window as the means of escape note.",
    "Rooflights to a garage roof to have a U-value not exceeding 2.2 W/m²K (Approved Document L Volume 1 Table 4.2), be fitted with a proprietary upstand kerb of not less than 150mm to a flat roof and flashed to the roof covering, and where they are the only means of purge ventilation be openable with an area of not less than one twentieth of the floor area. Internal doors from the new room to the house to be standard doorsets, other than the door to a retained garage and any door opening onto the protected stairway of a house with a floor more than 4.5m above ground, each of which is to be an FD30S doorset (E 30 Sa, with intumescent strips and cold smoke seals) fitted with a self-closer."]},
  {c:"Windows & Doors", t:"Security", p:[
    "Where the new window or door is on the ground floor and easily accessible, it is to be a secure door or window certificated to PAS 24:2016 or equivalent, with laminated glass to the door glazing, as recommended in Approved Document Q. Requirement Q1 applies only to new dwellings (including dwellings created by a material change of use) and not to a room added to an existing dwelling; the standard is specified here as good practice and to satisfy insurers."]},
  {c:"Ventilation", t:"Ventilation — Approved Document F", p:[
    "Ventilation to comply with Approved Document F Volume 1. The new habitable room is to have purge ventilation by openable windows or doors with an openable area of not less than one twentieth of the floor area where the opening light opens more than 30 degrees, or one tenth where it opens between 15 and 30 degrees (or a mechanical system giving four air changes per hour), and background ventilation by trickle ventilators in the window heads giving an equivalent area of not less than 8000mm² where the dwelling has more than one storey or 10,000mm² where it is single-storey, set not less than 1700mm above floor level, with the equivalent area marked or recorded and noise-attenuating ventilators used on facades exposed to traffic or other noise. Where the room is a bedroom, study or living room this is sufficient; the openable areas are to be checked against the window schedule before issue. Any internal door to the house is to be undercut 10mm above the finished floor (7600mm² for a 760mm door) to allow air movement between rooms.",
    "Where the new room becomes a kitchen, utility room, bathroom or WC, provide intermittent mechanical extract to BS EN 13141-4 at not less than 30 litres per second from a cooker hood over the hob (fitted 650mm to 750mm above it, to BS EN 13141-3) or 60 litres per second elsewhere in the kitchen, 30 litres per second from a utility room, 15 litres per second from a bathroom and 6 litres per second from a WC, with 4000mm² of background ventilation to the wet room; fans may be wired to the light switch and are to have a 15 minute overrun where the room has no openable window. Ductwork rigid wherever possible with flexible duct limited to 1.5m at final connections, installed to BSRIA BG 43, insulated where it passes through unheated voids and fire stopped or fitted with fire dampers where it passes through fire-resisting construction, discharging to outside air through a terminal with a back-draught shutter.",
    "Where the room has no external wall, or the dwelling as a whole is being brought to a design air permeability below 5 m³/(h·m²), continuous mechanical extract (System 3) is to be used with 4000mm² background ventilators to each habitable room and not fewer than the number of bedrooms plus two, or mechanical ventilation with heat recovery (System 4) with no background ventilators, supply terminals placed to avoid draughts and wet-room air not recirculated; continuous rates not less than 13 litres per second from the kitchen and 8 from each bathroom and utility room and 6 from a WC, and the whole-dwelling rate the greater of 0.3 litres per second per square metre and the bedroom-count rate. Every fixed mechanical ventilation system to be commissioned with the flow rates measured to BS EN 13141-6 or 13141-7 and the results given to building control within five days of the final test and to the occupier."]},
  {c:"Condensation (C2)", t:"Interstitial and Surface Condensation", p:[
    "The vapour control layer to the walls, floor and roof is to be continuous and on the warm side of the insulation, with all laps, junctions and service penetrations sealed. Internal insulation to a single-leaf wall is to be assessed for interstitial condensation to BS 5250 and BS EN ISO 13788 where the wall is exposed to driving rain or is of solid brick. Thermal bridging at the junction of the new floor, walls and roof is to be minimised by carrying the insulation continuously around the junctions and by insulated cavity closers at openings."]},
  {c:"Drainage", t:"Drainage — Connections, Depths, Chambers, Branch Pipes, Building Over Sewers and Pumped Drainage", p:[
    "New underground drainage in 100mm PVC-U to BS EN 1401 or vitrified clay laid at not less than 1:40, bedded on and surrounded by 100mm of pea gravel, with not less than 600mm cover under gardens and 900mm under drives and roads (1200mm for rigid pipes under roads), or protected by a reinforced concrete slab on a compressible layer with 75mm of granular material between the pipe and the slab where the cover is less. Flexible joints and rocker pipes not more than 600mm long each side of a wall or foundation with 50mm clearance in the opening, or the foundation carried down below the drain and the drain sleeved; pipes passing through the new infill wall or the existing walls to be sleeved with 50mm clearance and the sleeve fire stopped where the wall is fire-resisting. Inspection chambers at every change of direction or gradient, at junctions and at not more than 45m centres, 450mm diameter to 1.2m deep, with double-sealed bolt-down covers inside the building and vehicle-rated covers in drives; rodding eyes at the head of each run. Drains passing under a floor slab to have a 1m wide strip of A142 mesh in the slab over the run and a 150mm concrete surround where the crown is within 300mm of the underside of the slab. Existing drains passing under the garage are to be located before the foundation and slab work starts and protected in the same way.",
    "Where the new room includes a WC, utility or shower room, above-ground pipework to BS EN 12056-2 with 32mm minimum branches to basins, 40mm to showers and baths and 100mm to WCs, 75mm seal traps and branch lengths not exceeding 1.7m for a 32mm basin waste (3m at 40mm), 3m for a 40mm bath or shower waste (4m at 50mm) and 6m for a 100mm WC branch, beyond which anti-vacuum traps or a ventilated branch are needed; no branch is to connect within 200mm below a WC branch connection and the stack is to have a long-radius bend at its foot. Connect to the existing soil and vent pipe where it is within 6m, or to a new 100mm stack terminating 900mm above any opening within 3m, or fitted with an air admittance valve to BS EN 12380 above the highest spill-over level in an accessible ventilated position provided at least one stack on the drain remains open to the air; below ground, connect to the existing foul drain through a new inspection chamber or a saddle connection to the engineer's detail. Building over or within 3m of a public sewer, over a manhole, or where the sewer is more than 3m deep or larger than 225mm requires a build-over agreement with the sewerage undertaker with a CCTV survey before and after. A pumped WC or macerator is permitted only where a gravity WC is also available in the dwelling, with pumping stations to BS EN 12056-4 and BS EN 12050 with a non-return valve, alarm and 24 hours' storage. All new drains to be tested by air or water in the presence of building control before backfilling and again at completion."]},
  {c:"Services", t:"Electrical Installation", p:[
    "All electrical work to BS 7671 and Approved Document P, carried out by a registered competent person who is to issue an Electrical Installation Certificate and notify the work. Sockets, switches and controls between 450mm and 1200mm above floor level. Where the garage consumer unit or meter is within the new room it is to be enclosed in a non-combustible cupboard with ventilation, or relocated."]},
  {c:"Services", t:"Heating and Hot Water — Extending the Existing System, Boilers, Heat Pumps, Underfloor Heating and Part G", p:[
    "Extend the existing wet heating system with new radiators fitted with thermostatic radiator valves, or provide a new heat emitter served from the existing system, sized for the calculated heat loss of the new room and with the boiler output checked for the added load. Where the boiler is in the garage, confirm that the flue terminal position remains compliant with Approved Document J and the manufacturer's clearances after the infill wall and new window are built and that the appliance retains its combustion and ventilation air, and provide a carbon monoxide alarm to BS EN 50291 between 1m and 3m from the appliance where a combustion appliance remains in or opens onto the room. Heat producing appliances, flues and hearths to comply with Approved Document J; any new or extended gas installation to be by a Gas Safe registered engineer, oil by OFTEC and solid fuel by HETAS, each notifying the work and giving the commissioning certificate to building control.",
    "Where a new or replacement system is installed, wet heating systems are to be designed for a maximum flow temperature of 55°C with emitters sized accordingly. Gas boilers to have an ErP seasonal efficiency of not less than 92 per cent, a room-sealed flue with the terminal positioned to Approved Document J and the manufacturer's clearances and not less than 50mm from combustible material, inspection hatches of 300mm x 300mm at every joint and change of direction where a flue runs in a void, and, for a combination boiler, one of the additional efficiency measures in the Domestic Building Services Compliance Guide (flue gas heat recovery, weather or load compensation, or a smart thermostat with automation and optimisation). Controls to Approved Document L Volume 1 Section 5: time and temperature control of every heating zone, two zones where the dwelling exceeds 150m², thermostatic radiator valves, a cylinder thermostat and time control for hot water independent of the heating, and a boiler interlock; primary and accessible pipework, and all pipework outside the heated envelope, insulated to Table 4.4 of Approved Document L Volume 1 and the Domestic Building Services Compliance Guide.",
    "Heat pumps to MCS MIS 3005 with the heat loss calculation, emitter and cylinder sizing and the outdoor unit sited to the MCS 020 noise limit at the nearest neighbour's window; any back-up heater interlocked so it never leads. Oil-fired appliances installed by an OFTEC registered technician with the oil tank not less than 1.8m from the building, 760mm from the boundary, on a fire-resisting base extending 300mm beyond the tank, bunded to 110 per cent where there is a pollution risk, and fitted with a fire valve. Electric storage heaters with automatic charge control and panel heaters with time and temperature control in each room; electric underfloor heating on a dedicated circuit with 30mA RCD protection and a floor sensor thermostat to each zone, with continuity and insulation tests recorded before the floor is covered.",
    "Wet underfloor heating designed to BS EN 1264 with the floor surface temperature limited to 29°C (27 with resilient finishes), insulation beneath the pipes of not less than ten times the resistance of the floor finish (R 0.75 m²K/W minimum on intermediate floors), 25mm edge insulation, a 65mm minimum screed over the pipes with movement joints at doorways, zone boundaries and bays over 40m² or 8m long, the system pressure tested before the screed is placed and commissioned to BS EN 1264-4 with the manifold zones labelled. Hot water storage to Approved Document G3 with the cylinder thermostat set so that stored water is held at not less than 60°C; unvented storage to BS EN 12897 installed by a person registered with a competent person scheme for unvented hot water, with the temperature and pressure relief valve, expansion vessel and non-self-resetting thermal cut-out, a tundish within 500mm of the valve, the discharge pipe one size larger than the valve outlet routed as D1/D2 to a safe visible termination, and a warning label; hot water to baths limited to 48°C by a thermostatic mixing valve to BS EN 1111 or BS EN 1287. The commissioning certificate and the Benchmark record for every appliance to be given to building control and the occupier."]},
  {c:"Conservation of Fuel & Power", t:"Thermal Elements and U-values", p:[
    "The garage walls, floor and roof become thermal elements of the dwelling on conversion and are to achieve the values in Approved Document L Volume 1 Table 4.3 for retained thermal elements: walls 0.30 W/m²K (0.55 where a cavity wall is upgraded by cavity fill alone), floors 0.25 W/m²K and roofs of every type 0.16 W/m²K. New elements, being the infill wall and any new floor or roof, are to achieve the values in Table 4.2 for new elements in existing dwellings: walls 0.18, floors 0.18 and roofs 0.15 W/m²K, windows and doors 1.4 W/m²K.",
    "The build-ups in Part A are calculated to BS EN ISO 6946 and BS EN ISO 13370 with the manufacturers' declared conductivities current at the date of issue; where an alternative product is used the calculation is to be repeated and submitted to building control.",
    "NOTE — Approved Documents L1 and F1, 2026 editions, come into force on 24 March 2027. Work with a full plans application submitted before that date remains under the current standards provided work commences before 24 March 2028. The values for work to existing dwellings are expected to carry forward; confirm against the edition in force at the date of submission."]},
  {c:"Conservation of Fuel & Power", t:"Fixed Lighting — Internal and External", p:[
    "All new fixed internal and external lighting to the converted room to have a luminous efficacy of not less than 75 lumens per circuit-watt (Approved Document L Volume 1, 2021 edition, Section 6 and the Domestic Building Services Compliance Guide), with LED luminaires or lamps throughout; fittings rated at less than 5 circuit-watts are excluded from the count. Where the existing garage lighting is retained it is to be replaced or upgraded to the same standard, because the space is a new heated part of the dwelling and Part L applies to it.",
    "Light fittings recessed into the new insulated ceiling to be fire-rated or fitted with fire hoods, sealed to the vapour control layer so that the air barrier is not perforated, and kept clear of insulation as the manufacturer requires. Fixed external lighting to the new door or window openings to be controlled so that it switches off automatically when there is enough daylight and when not required at night, by a photocell with a PIR or a time switch, and to meet the same efficacy figure. Circuits to Approved Document P, with outdoor fittings of an IP rating suitable for their exposure."
   ]},
  {c:"Conservation of Fuel & Power", t:"Airtightness and Continuity", p:[
    "Seal the junctions between the new floor deck, wall linings and ceiling with a flexible sealant or airtightness tape, seal around window and door frames internally, and seal all service penetrations. A pressure test is not required for a conversion but the standard of workmanship is to be that expected for a new dwelling."]},
  {c:"Roofs", t:"Leadwork, Flashings and Valleys", p:[
    "Leadwork to BS EN 12588 and the Lead Sheet Training Academy's Rolled Lead Sheet Manual: Code 4 for soakers, cover flashings and small aprons, Code 5 for valley gutters, back gutters and the flat areas of dormer roofs, and Code 6 for wide gutters, with piece lengths limited to 1.5m and the laps, upstands and expansion allowances in the manual; stepped and cover flashings tucked 25mm into the masonry joint and wedged, a slip layer between mortar and lead, lead flashings beneath window sills where the sill has no drip, and patination oil applied to new lead on completion. Valleys as lead-lined or GRP valleys on 19mm x 225mm valley boards with an exposed channel of not less than 100mm (125mm below 30 degrees pitch) and a 100mm lap between lengths, or a proprietary dry valley where the pitch and tile allow; tiles bedded on fillets clear of the lead. Where lead is in contact with oak or a copper-containing alloy it is to be isolated with a building paper. Lead substitutes may be used where the planning authority accepts them and the product carries a BBA certificate for the application."]},
  {c:"Means of Escape & Fire Regs", t:"External Fire Spread — Boundaries, Unprotected Areas and Roof Coverings", p:[
    "External walls within 1m of the relevant boundary are to have 30 minutes fire resistance from both sides (60 minutes where they separate buildings), an external surface of Class B-s3, d2 or better, and unprotected areas (windows, doors and combustible cladding) limited to small areas not exceeding 1m² and not less than 4m apart. Walls more than 1m from the boundary may have unprotected areas up to the limits in Approved Document B Volume 1 Section 11 for a residential building: 5.6m² at 1m, 12m² at 2m, 18m² at 3m, 24m² at 4m, 30m² at 5m and unlimited at 6m or more, measured for each side of the building facing that boundary; where cladding is worse than Class B-s3, d2 and more than 1mm thick, half its area counts as unprotected. The relevant boundary is the site boundary or the centre line of an adjoining road, canal or river.",
    "Roof coverings within 6m of the boundary are to be classified BROOF(t4) to BS EN 13501-5, which tiles, slates, felt on a substrate, single-ply membranes, GRP systems and glass with the appropriate classification achieve; thermoplastic rooflights are limited in area and distance as Section 12 and are not to be within 6m of the boundary unless classified. The classification of the roof covering, the rooflights and any cladding is to be stated on the drawings, and the position of the relevant boundary shown on the site plan so building control can check the unprotected area calculation."]},
  {c:"Drainage", t:"Off-Mains Foul Drainage — Treatment Plants, Septic Tanks and Drainage Fields", p:[
    "Where no public sewer is within reasonable distance, foul drainage is to discharge to a packaged treatment plant to BS EN 12566-3 sized to British Water's Flows and Loads for the number of bedrooms plus two persons, capable of operating for six hours without power, sited not less than 7m from the dwelling and within 30m of a hardstanding for the desludging tanker, with the effluent discharged to a drainage field designed to BS 6297 on three percolation tests, laid in trenches 300mm to 900mm wide at not more than 700mm deep on a 300mm bed of clean shingle with the pipes at a gradient not steeper than 1:200 and not less than 2m between trenches, sited at least 15m from any building, 10m from a watercourse or permeable drain and 50m from a well, spring or borehole, with the water table not less than 1m below the invert and no paving or traffic over it. Discharge to a watercourse is only permitted under the Environment Agency's general binding rules or an environmental permit, and new septic tanks discharging to a watercourse are not permitted.",
    "Where a septic tank is still acceptable with a drainage field it is to have a capacity of not less than 2700 litres plus 180 litres for each user over four, sited 7m from the dwelling with access for emptying, and a cesspool is a last resort requiring the local authority's agreement with a capacity of 18,000 litres for two users plus 6,800 litres per additional user. The installation is to be registered with the Environment Agency where required and the maintenance schedule handed to the occupier."]},
  {c:"Services", t:"Chimneys, Flues, Hearths and Stoves (Part J)", p:[
    "Any new chimney, flue, hearth or solid fuel appliance is to comply with Approved Document J. A masonry chimney to have a lined flue to BS EN 1457 or a flexible or rigid metal liner to BS EN 1856 of the diameter the appliance requires (not less than 150mm for a closed appliance up to 30kW, 200mm for an open fire), backfilled with insulating concrete, with not more than two offsets at not more than 45 degrees, combustible material kept 200mm from the inner face of the flue and 40mm from the outer face of the chimney, and the chimney height not more than 4.5 times its least width unless designed. Flue outlets to terminate not less than 600mm above the ridge where within 600mm of it, 2.3m horizontally from and 1m above any adjacent roof surface or opening, and clear of the boundary as Diagram 3.4. Single-wall flue pipes for stoves to be kept three times their diameter from combustibles, or 1.5 times with a shield, and to have 300mm x 300mm access panels where they pass through voids.",
    "Hearths of 125mm concrete extending not less than 500mm in front of and 150mm each side of a fireplace opening, or a superimposed hearth of not less than 12mm for a stove whose base does not exceed 100°C, with the hearth edge clearly apparent; fireplace recess walls 200mm thick at the sides where separating dwellings and 100mm at the back. A permanently open air supply of 550mm² for each kilowatt over 5kW for a stove, more where an extract fan is in the same room (a fan is not to be fitted in the room of an open-flued appliance without a spillage test), a register plate where a liner enters an existing chimney, a carbon monoxide alarm to BS EN 50291 in the room, and a notice plate fixed beside the meter or consumer unit recording the flue and hearth and the appliance they suit. Installation by a HETAS or equivalent registered installer who notifies the work, or by building notice with a smoke and spillage test on completion, and the Approved Document J Appendix A checklist completed."]},
 ]};
